 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples, as each sample is processed once [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of rays and the depth of the FIFOs/Registers, which depend on the volume size and view angle [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles non-ray-order sample presentation efficiently in hardware; supports multiple blending modes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires significant hardware resources for FIFOs and alignment logic; precision loss can occur if bit depth is insufficient for large volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time ray casting on hardware architectures like VolumePro [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample, as compositing is a fixed-function hardware operation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the depth of the volume and the angle of the rays, requiring storage proportional to the number of active rays in the FIFOs and shift registers [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Designed for real-time ray casting, as implemented in the VolumePro system [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles non-ordered sample streams efficiently without software sorting; hardware acceleration ensures low latency [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires specific hardware support (FIFOs, MUXes, Ray Alignment unit); precision is limited to 12 bits for intermediate values [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering semi-transparent volumes with hardware acceleration and non-ordered sample generation (e.g., slice-by-slice rendering) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When software-only rendering is required or when higher precision than 12 bits is critical for intermediate compositing steps [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Ray Compositing and Accumulation was developed as part of the VolumePro real-time ray-casting system [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to handle the specific data flow of slice-by-slice, beam-by-beam rendering on dedicated hardware [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the challenge of maintaining correct compositing order when samples are not generated in strict ray order [S1, unofficial].
+The method was described in the context of the VolumePro real-time ray-casting system [S1, unofficial]. VolumePro is a specialized hardware architecture for volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. The specific section on accumulating color values along rays details the hardware mechanisms (Ray Slice FIFO, Ray Beam FIFO, Ray Shift Register) used to manage the compositing process for samples that are not presented in ray order [S1, unofficial].
 
